<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demetrios Palaiologos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1407–1470</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demetrios Palaiologos was a younger brother of the last Byzantine emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a>, and despot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> from 1449 to 1460. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> he quarrelled and fought with his brother Thomas, who shared the despotate, and both faced Albanian revolts. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> invaded in 1460 Demetrios surrendered to him; he died as a monk in Adrianople.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demetrios-palaiologos.jpg" alt="Demetrios Palaiologos" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown. The designs of the seals date to the 15th century, the modern photgraphic sketches are by an unknown creator. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%A3%CF%86%CF%81%CE%B1%CE%B3%CE%AF%CF%82_%CE%94%CE%B7%CE%BC%CE%B7%CF%84%CF%81%CE%AF%CE%BF%CF%85_%CE%A0%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%B1%CE%B9%CE%BF%CE%BB%CF%8C%CE%B3%CE%BF%CF%85.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
